  },
  "presentation_summary": [
    "57-year-old male agricultural worker with type 2 diabetes and arterial hypertension had high-grade fever, arthralgia, asthenia, and vomiting for eight days before hospital admission.",
    "On day 8 he developed psychomotor agitation, behavioral changes, and seizures; non-contrast CT brain was normal.",
    "Initial CSF showed pleocytosis 240 cells/mm3 with 72% neutrophils, normal glucose, and high protein.",
    "At hospital admission he had hypotension, bradycardia, reduced consciousness with Glasgow Coma Scale 11, then severe dyspnea requiring prompt intubation, mechanical ventilation, and continuous sedation.",
    "Laboratory tests revealed renal dysfunction treated conservatively without hemodialysis.",
    "MRI on day 2 showed frontal, parietal, mesial bilateral temporal cortical and subcortical altered signals with restricted diffusion plus posterior thalamus, midbrain, and cerebellar vermis abnormalities, compatible with diffuse encephalitis.",
    "Further CSF on day 12 showed pleocytosis 98 cells/mm3 with 68% lymphocytes and 12% monocytes, protein 161 mg/dL, glucose 81 mg/dL, and high ADA 47.9 IU/L.",
    "CSF and serum were positive for IgM antibodies to chikungunya virus; syphilis, HIV antibodies, Mycobacterium tuberculosis PCR, and HSV-1/HSV-2 PCR were negative.",
    "Despite antimicrobial therapy, acyclovir, antiepileptic drugs, ventilation, and supportive care, he remained comatose and died on day 24 after illness onset."
  ],
